---
/**
 * Footer — the back cover.
 *
 * This used to be a conventional four-column sitemap: nav groups, socials,
 * legal row. That is a *directory*, and a directory at the bottom of a page
 * is a thing you install because footers are supposed to have one, not
 * because anybody scrolls down hunting for it. The header rail is this
 * site's actual navigation.
 *
 * So the footer is a colophon now — the back cover of the printed object the
 * rest of the site is pretending to be. Three things, in the order a back
 * cover puts them:
 *
 *   1. THE OFFER      "Stay in the loop" + the capture. The one action.
 *   2. THE FINE PRINT one hairline row of tracked micro-labels.
 *   3. THE IMPRINT    the wordmark, set enormous, edge to edge.
 *
 * WHY THE WORDMARK IS LAST. A page has to end on something. If the last
 * thing is a link list, the page ends on admin. Ending on the name — at a
 * size that can only be read as a signature rather than as information —
 * makes the bottom of the page a destination instead of a stopping point.
 * It is the move both reference sites make, and it is why their footers feel
 * like arriving somewhere rather than running out of road.
 *
 * SEAMLESSNESS. Deliberately no top divider and no ground change: the hero
 * ends on black and the footer starts on black, so there is no seam to see.
 * The transition is carried by SCALE, not by a rule or a colour switch — you
 * fall out of a full-viewport plate into a piece of type big enough to read
 * as architecture. A colour change here (the obvious move, and what both
 * references do — they flood the footer with their brand colour) would be a
 * hard cut, which is the opposite of what was asked for. If that version is
 * ever wanted instead, it is one class: put `bg-brand` on the <footer> and
 * the whole thing becomes the site's one full blue field.
 *
 * The wordmark uses the header logo's exact left padding, so the tiny mark
 * that opens the page and the enormous one that closes it start on the same
 * vertical line.
 *
 * Sizing is a calc() against the AVAILABLE width, not a clamp and not a raw
 * vw. A clamp would cap the mark on wide screens and leave a gutter that
 * reads as under-set rather than deliberate. A raw vw looks right on desktop
 * and then overflows on phones, because the side padding is a fixed pixel
 * value and is therefore a much larger *share* of a 375px viewport than of a
 * 1440px one — the measured overflow at 375px was about 4%, clipped silently
 * by the overflow-hidden. Dividing (viewport − padding) by the string's
 * own width-to-font-size ratio makes it span the measure at every width
 * instead, which is the entire point of the element.
 */
import Section from "../ui/Section.astro";
import Container from "../ui/Container.astro";
import NewsletterForm from "../forms/NewsletterForm.astro";
import { site } from "../../lib/site";

const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---

<footer>
  {/* 1 — THE OFFER. No `divider`: the seam between hero and footer is the one
      thing this component must not have. */}
  <Section space="md">
    <Container width="wide">
      <div class="grid items-center gap-10 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-[length:var(--text-display-md)]">Stay in the loop</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-sm text-content-muted">
            Occasional notes on pharmacology, plus first access to new cohorts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <NewsletterForm class="max-w-md md:justify-self-end" />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  {/* 2 — THE FINE PRINT. Everything legally obliged to exist and nothing that
      isn't, set as metadata rather than as navigation. */}
  <div
    class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-t border-border px-[18px] py-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between md:px-[34px]"
  >
    <p class="label text-content-faint">&copy; {year} {site.name}</p>

    <ul class="flex gap-6">
      <li>
        <a href="/legal/privacy" class="label text-content-faint transition-colors hover:text-chalk">Privacy</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/legal/terms" class="label text-content-faint transition-colors hover:text-chalk">Terms</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  {/* 3 — THE IMPRINT. `aria-hidden` because this is the site name repeated as
      an image, not as information: a screen reader already had it from the
      header logo and from the copyright line directly above, and a third
      reading is noise. `select-none` for the same reason — it is a mark, not
      text anyone would copy.

      The line-height is measured, not guessed. Mondwest's ink bounds for
      this string are 0.759em above the baseline and 0.250em below it —
      1.009em of actual glyph — while a default line box adds a band of dead
      space under the descenders that would leave the mark floating short of
      the page edge. Setting leading to exactly the ink height puts the tips
      of the p, g and y ON the bottom edge with nothing beneath them.

      It sits flush and COMPLETE rather than bleeding off the edge. Cropping
      is the more dramatic option and it is what the reference does, but the
      reference crops all-caps with no descenders; cutting through the
      descenders of a lowercase wordmark reads as a mistake rather than as a
      decision, and it costs the one thing this element exists to say. */}
  <div class="overflow-hidden px-[18px] md:px-[34px]" aria-hidden="true">
    <p
      class="select-none whitespace-nowrap text-[calc((100vw-36px)/9.3)] leading-[1.009] tracking-[-0.01em] text-chalk md:text-[calc((100vw-68px)/9.3)]"
    >
      {site.name}
    </p>
  </div>
</footer>
